## Case: call-source-order
id: "av.parity.1080.call-source-order"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1080", "calls"]
links: ["PLAN-1080"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
fn mark(n int) int { print(n); return n }
fn sub(a int, b int) int { return a * 10 + b }
fn main() int { return sub(b: mark(2), a: mark(1)) }
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
exit: 12

#### stdout

```text
2
1
```

### Notes

CALL-SOURCE-ORDER: the named argument expressions evaluate in SOURCE
order (stdout `2` then `1`) while the binding is by name (a=1, b=2 →
value 12). The trace channel is the console (module globals are not part
of this batch); the assertion observes both streams, not only the final
value.

## Case: pipe-legacy-m10
id: "av.parity.1080.pipe-legacy-m10"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1080", "calls"]
links: ["PLAN-1080", "PLAN-514"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
type Pipe10 {
    v int
    fn add(n int) Pipe10 { return Pipe10.new(.v + n) }
    fn get() int { return .v }
    static fn new(v int) Pipe10 { return Pipe10(v) }
}
fn main() {
    print(Pipe10.new(1) |> .add(2) |> .get())
    var x = Pipe10.new(5)
    |> .add(3)
    |> .add(10)
    print(x.get())
    print(x |> .v)
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
channel: "unit"

#### stdout

```text
3
18
18
```

### VM result
#ignore
check-id: "legacy-reference"

```text
3
18
18
```

### Notes

The legacy m10_pipe source byte-for-byte (its original oracle
`3\n18\n18`): static constructor calls, receiver `.v` reads, line-start
`|>` continuations and the field-projection pipe, on the ordinary AV
chain. The void `fn main()` is the Unit entry (the worker's unit
channel).

